<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-external-force-examples" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Two Examples of the Forced System</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO PROSE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    We now solve the forced undamped system for two different values of the spring
    constant <m>k</m>. The method is undetermined coefficients, which you practiced
    in <xref ref="sec-undetermined-duplication"/> and surrounding sections, so feel
    free to skim the algebraic details in parts (b) and (c). What matters most is
    understanding which case you are in and what the general solution looks like at the end.
  </p>

  <p>
    Throughout both examples we use <m>m = 1</m>, <m>F_0 = 50</m>, <m>G_0 = 0</m>,
    and <m>\omega = 3</m>, so the ODE is
    <me>x'' + kx = 50\cos(3t),</me>
    and we find the general solution for two choices of <m>k</m>: first <m>k = 8</m>,
    then <m>k = 9</m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="activity-forced-examples">
    <title>Solving the Forced System for Two Values of <m>k</m></title>

    <!-- ── Part (a): MC on which case is duplication ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-forced-which-case">
      <title>Part (a): Which example is the case of duplication?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which of the two examples is in the case of duplication?
          (See <xref ref="thm-case-of-duplication"/> for a reminder.)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The example with <m>k = 9</m>, because the homogeneous equation becomes
              <m>x'' + 9x = 0</m>, i.e. <m>x'' + 3^2 x = 0</m>, so <m>x_c</m> contains
              <m>\cos(3t)</m> and <m>\sin(3t)</m>. The standard guess <m>A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)</m>
              already solves the homogeneous equation, giving us the case of duplication.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! When <m>k = 9</m> the homogeneous equation is <m>x'' + 3^2 x = 0</m>,
              so <m>x_c</m> already contains <m>\cos(3t)</m> and <m>\sin(3t)</m>. The standard
              guess duplicates <m>x_c</m> and we must multiply by <m>t</m>.
              Note that this happened because <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{9} = 3 = \omega</m>:
              the natural frequency equals the forcing frequency.
              <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The example with <m>k = 8</m>, because <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{8} \neq 3</m>
              makes the algebra messier.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Messier algebra does not mean duplication. The case of duplication is about
              whether the guess for <m>x_p</m> already appears in <m>x_c</m>, which depends
              on whether <m>\omega_0 = \omega</m>, not on how clean the numbers are.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Both examples are cases of duplication, since both have cosine on the right-hand side.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Having cosine on the right-hand side is not enough to cause duplication.
              Duplication happens only when the frequency of that cosine matches
              <m>\omega_0</m>. That only occurs when <m>k = 9</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Neither example is a case of duplication.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              When <m>k = 9</m>, the natural frequency is <m>\omega_0 = 3</m>, which equals
              the forcing frequency <m>\omega = 3</m>. So <m>\cos(3t)</m> and <m>\sin(3t)</m>
              already appear in <m>x_c</m>, and we do have duplication.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <p>
      Notice what triggered the duplication: the natural frequency <m>\omega_0</m> and the
      forcing frequency <m>\omega</m> happened to be equal when <m>k = 9</m>. When they differ,
      as with <m>k = 8</m>, there is no duplication and the standard guess works fine.
      Keep this in mind as you work through the solutions below.
    </p>

    <!-- ── Part (b): Full solution for k=8 ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-forced-solve-k8">
      <title>Part (b): General solution when <m>k = 8</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the general solution to <m>x'' + 8x = 50\cos(3t)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Complementary solution:</alert>
          <me>x_c = c_1\cos(\sqrt{8}\,t) + c_2\sin(\sqrt{8}\,t).</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Particular solution:</alert>
          <me>x_p = -50\cos(3t).</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>General solution:</alert>
          <me>x(t) = c_1\cos(\sqrt{8}\,t) + c_2\sin(\sqrt{8}\,t) - 50\cos(3t).</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <title>Finding <m>x_p</m> (can skim)</title>
        <p>
          Since <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{8} \neq 3 = \omega</m>, this is the normal case.
          We guess <m>x_p = A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)</m> and compute:
          <md>
            <mrow>x_p' \amp= -3A\sin(3t) + 3B\cos(3t)</mrow>
            <mrow>x_p'' \amp= -9A\cos(3t) - 9B\sin(3t)</mrow>
          </md>
          Substituting into <m>x'' + 8x</m> and collecting:
          <me>-A\cos(3t) - B\sin(3t) = 50\cos(3t).</me>
        </p>
        <solution>
          <title>Matching coefficients (can skim)</title>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow>-A \amp= 50 \implies A = -50</mrow>
              <mrow>-B \amp= 0 \implies B = 0</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
        <p>
          So <m>x_p = -50\cos(3t)</m>. Note that <m>B = 0</m> because there is no
          <m>x'</m> term in the ODE and the right-hand side has no sine.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- ── Part (c): Full solution for k=9 ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-forced-solve-k9">
      <title>Part (c): General solution when <m>k = 9</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the general solution to <m>x'' + 9x = 50\cos(3t)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Complementary solution:</alert>
          <me>x_c = c_1\cos(3t) + c_2\sin(3t).</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Particular solution:</alert>
          <me>x_p = \frac{50}{6}\,t\sin(3t).</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>General solution:</alert>
          <me>x(t) = c_1\cos(3t) + c_2\sin(3t) + \frac{50}{6}\,t\sin(3t).</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <title>Finding <m>x_p</m> (can skim)</title>
        <p>
          Since <m>\omega_0 = 3 = \omega</m>, we have duplication. The modified guess is
          <m>x_p = t\bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr)</m>. Computing derivatives by the product rule:
          <md>
            <mrow>x_p' \amp= \bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr) + t\bigl(-3A\sin(3t) + 3B\cos(3t)\bigr)</mrow>
            <mrow>x_p'' \amp= -6A\sin(3t) + 6B\cos(3t) + t\bigl(-9A\cos(3t) - 9B\sin(3t)\bigr)</mrow>
          </md>
          Substituting into <m>x'' + 9x</m>, the <m>t</m> terms cancel and we get:
          <me>-6A\sin(3t) + 6B\cos(3t) = 50\cos(3t).</me>
        </p>
        <solution>
          <title>Matching coefficients (can skim)</title>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow>6B \amp= 50 \implies B = \tfrac{50}{6}</mrow>
              <mrow>-6A \amp= 0 \implies A = 0</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
        <p>
          So <m>x_p = \tfrac{50}{6}\,t\sin(3t)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLOSING MC: COMPARING THE TWO EXAMPLES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Look at the two general solutions side by side:
    <md>
      <mrow>k = 8: \quad x(t) \amp= c_1\cos(\sqrt{8}\,t) + c_2\sin(\sqrt{8}\,t) - 50\cos(3t)</mrow>
      <mrow>k = 9: \quad x(t) \amp= c_1\cos(3t) + c_2\sin(3t) + \tfrac{50}{6}\,t\sin(3t)</mrow>
    </md>
    They look quite different. What was it about the setup that caused this?
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-frequency-comparison">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is fundamentally different between the two examples that led to such different solutions?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            In the first example <m>\omega = 3</m> and <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{8}</m> are different,
            so we get a standard particular solution. In the second example <m>\omega = \omega_0 = 3</m>,
            which puts us in the case of duplication and introduces the factor of <m>t</m> in <m>x_p</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Exactly. When <m>\omega \neq \omega_0</m> (as with <m>k = 8</m>) we get
            the standard case and a bounded particular solution. When <m>\omega = \omega_0</m>
            (as with <m>k = 9</m>) the homogeneous equation takes the form
            <m>x'' + \omega^2 x = 0</m>, so the standard guess already solves it:
            we are in the case of duplication, the factor of <m>t</m> appears in <m>x_p</m>,
            and the two solutions look completely different. On the next page we see
            what this means physically.
            <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The second example had a larger value of <m>k</m>, which made the ODE harder to solve.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The size of <m>k</m> alone is not what caused the difference. What mattered
            was the relationship between <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k}</m> and the forcing
            frequency <m>\omega</m>: specifically, whether they were equal or not.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The second example required the duplication rule because the right-hand side
            was more complicated.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The right-hand side is <m>50\cos(3t)</m> in both examples. The duplication
            arose because the natural frequency <m>\omega_0 = 3</m> happened to match
            the forcing frequency <m>\omega = 3</m>, not because of anything different
            about the right-hand side.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In the first example <m>B = 0</m> dropped out, making it a simpler calculation.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That <m>B = 0</m> in the first example is a nice simplification, but it is
            not the fundamental difference between the two cases. The real distinction
            is whether the forcing frequency matches the natural frequency.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Two cases:</alert> When the forcing frequency <m>\omega</m> and the
          natural frequency <m>\omega_0</m> are different, undetermined coefficients
          works in the standard way. When <m>\omega = \omega_0</m>, we hit the case of
          duplication and must multiply the guess by <m>t</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Frequencies are the key:</alert> The case of duplication is not about
          the algebra being harder; it arises precisely because the external force is
          driving the system at its own natural frequency. On the next page we see what
          this means physically.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

